    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Tooth count is the number tool brands compete on hardest and explain least. It is genuinely useful — it decides whether you can turn a bolt in a two-inch gap — but more is not simply better, because every tooth you add makes each tooth smaller and the mechanism behind it finer.
        </p>

        <div id="what-teeth-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the Teeth Actually Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inside a ratchet head is a toothed gear attached to the drive square, and a spring-loaded pawl that drops into those teeth. Turn the handle one way and the pawl locks into a tooth, transmitting torque to the fastener. Turn it back and the pawl rides over the teeth with the familiar clicking, leaving the fastener where it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tooth count is simply how many teeth are cut into that gear. A 72-tooth ratchet has 72 of them around the circumference; a 90-tooth ratchet has 90 smaller ones in the same space.
          </p>

          <p>
            That count determines one thing directly: how far you must swing the handle before the pawl finds the next tooth and can take load again. More teeth means a shorter swing, which matters enormously when something is in the way.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth being clear that tooth count changes nothing about how much torque the ratchet can ultimately apply in the working direction, up to the point where the mechanism itself is the weak link — which is exactly where the trade-off lives, and we come to it below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Arithmetic</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Swing arc = 360 degrees divided by the tooth count.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 36-tooth ratchet needs 10 degrees of movement to catch the next tooth. A 72-tooth needs 5. A 90-tooth needs 4. In open air that difference is irrelevant; in a gap between a bracket and a pipe it is the difference between the job being possible and not.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="swing-arc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Swing Arc Is the Number That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tooth count is a marketing number. Swing arc is the same number expressed as what you will actually experience, and it is the one to compare.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tooth Count</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Swing Arc</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">In Practice</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24 teeth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 degrees</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Basic and cheap; struggles in tight work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">36 teeth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 degrees</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common on budget sets; fine in open access</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">72 teeth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 degrees</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The sensible standard for most users</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">90 teeth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 degrees</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Noticeably better in confined spaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">120 teeth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 degrees</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Specialist; finest mechanism, least robust</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The gains are not linear, and that is the key insight. Going from 36 to 72 teeth halves the required swing — a large, immediately obvious improvement. Going from 72 to 90 saves one further degree. Going from 90 to 120 saves one more.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the meaningful upgrade is escaping the low counts. Once you are at 72, additional teeth deliver diminishing returns while the mechanism gets progressively more delicate, which is why a great many professional mechanics carry 72 or 90 rather than chasing the highest number on the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the fine counts genuinely earn their keep is work where the obstruction is permanent — inside engine bays, behind dashboards, in machinery — and where you cannot solve the problem by moving to a different tool position. If that is most of your work, the extra degree is worth having.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-trade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Strength Trade Nobody Advertises
          </h2>

          <p>
            The gear inside a ratchet head is a fixed size, set by how big the head can be while still fitting where you need it. Cutting more teeth into that same circumference means each tooth is smaller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smaller teeth have less material at their base, so each one carries the load over a smaller area. The pawl engages less depth of tooth. Under very high torque — leaning on a seized fastener, or using a cheater bar — a fine-tooth ratchet is more likely to skip, and skipping under load is both how knuckles get hurt and how a ratchet's teeth get rounded off permanently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers compensate with better steel, heat treatment and pawl design, and good fine-tooth ratchets are genuinely strong. But the compensation costs money, which is why a cheap 90-tooth ratchet is a worse tool than a good 72-tooth one: the budget version has the finer teeth without the metallurgy to support them.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a dirt consideration. Finer mechanisms have tighter tolerances and less clearance, so grit, swarf and old grease affect them sooner. A coarse ratchet tolerates a filthy workshop in a way a 120-tooth mechanism does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical conclusion: for breaking fasteners loose, use a breaker bar rather than any ratchet, and let the ratchet do what it is good at — spinning fasteners quickly once they are moving. That habit makes tooth count a pure convenience question rather than a durability one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="common-counts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Comparing the Counts You Will Actually See
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four counts cover nearly everything on sale, and each has a natural buyer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">24 to 36 Teeth</h3>

          <p>
            Found on budget sets and older tools. A 10 to 15 degree swing is fine in open access — a workbench, a bicycle, most household jobs — and these mechanisms are robust and tolerant of neglect. If your work is not confined, this is not the limitation people claim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">72 Teeth</h3>

          <p>
            The practical standard, and the count we would recommend to most buyers. Five degrees handles the overwhelming majority of tight spots, the teeth are still substantial, and it is available on well-made tools at sensible prices.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">90 Teeth</h3>

          <p>
            A genuine improvement for automotive and machinery work where confined access is routine. Worth paying for from a manufacturer whose steel and heat treatment you trust; not worth it as the headline feature on a cheap set.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">120 Teeth and Above</h3>

          <p>
            Specialist tools for very restricted work. The mechanism is fine enough that it deserves care, clean conditions and a breaker bar kept nearby for anything stubborn. Excellent in the right hands and the wrong first purchase.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What to Buy for Your Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the count to how confined your typical job is, then buy the best-made example you can at that count rather than moving up a count on a cheaper tool.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Work</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tooth Count</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Household DIY, flat-pack, bikes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36-72</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Access is rarely the constraint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">General car maintenance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72-90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Engine bays are genuinely tight</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Engine and transmission work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90-120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Obstruction everywhere, low torque per fastener</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wheels, suspension, heavy fasteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 plus a breaker bar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strength matters more than swing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appliance and machinery repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Confined, low torque, awkward angles</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice the fourth row. For heavy fasteners the right answer is not a finer ratchet but a breaker bar — a solid bar with no mechanism at all, which cannot skip because there is nothing inside it to skip. Break the fastener loose with that, then switch to the ratchet to spin it out.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying a complete set, the ratchet is the component to scrutinise, because it is the part that fails. Sockets are simple lumps of steel and last decades; the ratchet has springs, a pawl and fine teeth, and a bad one is the reason people replace sets. Our <a href="/best-tekton-hand-tools/">hand tool guide</a> weighs the ratchets specifically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="build-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tooth Count Is Not Build Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            A high tooth count on a cheap ratchet tells you the manufacturer cut more teeth, not that they built a better tool. Several things matter more and none of them appear on the front of the packaging.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pawl design and spring quality.</strong> Whether the pawl engages fully and consistently, and whether the spring keeps doing so after a few thousand cycles. This is the single most common failure point and it is invisible until it fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Steel and heat treatment.</strong> Fine teeth need hard, tough steel to survive. The same tooth count in two ratchets can differ enormously in how long the teeth hold their shape.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sealing and serviceability.</strong> Whether dirt gets into the mechanism, and whether you can open it to clean and regrease it. A ratchet you can service outlives one you cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The warranty and whether it is honoured.</strong> Hand tool brands offering genuine lifetime replacement on ratchets are making a statement about expected failure rates. That promise tells you more than a tooth count does, and it is worth checking how the claim process actually works before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

          <p>
            One feature worth more than a tooth count upgrade on many jobs: a flex-head ratchet. Being able to angle the head twenty or thirty degrees relative to the handle often gets you onto a fastener that no amount of swing-arc refinement would have reached, because the obstruction was in the path of the handle rather than in the arc of its travel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Similarly, a thin-profile head matters as much as the mechanism inside it. Two 90-tooth ratchets from different makers can differ by several millimetres in head thickness, and in the sort of gap where tooth count is supposed to help, those millimetres decide whether the tool goes on the bolt at all. Handle both before buying if you can, and look at the head rather than the sticker.
          </p>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Keeping a Ratchet Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ratchets are the part of a socket set that wears out, and nearly all of that wear is preventable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the mechanism clean and greased. Most ratchets have a removable cover held by a snap ring or small screws; once a year, open it, wipe the old grease and grit out, and repack it with a light grease. A mechanism full of abrasive dust wears its own teeth, and that is what turns a smooth ratchet into one that skips.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use a ratchet as a breaker bar. This is the single biggest cause of damage — loading the pawl beyond its design and rounding the tooth it is sitting on. Once a tooth is rounded, the ratchet will skip at that position forever, and no amount of cleaning brings it back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never extend the handle with a pipe. The extra leverage goes straight into the mechanism, and the failure is sudden. If you need more leverage, the tool for that job is a longer breaker bar, which is designed for exactly this and costs very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, store ratchets dry and set the direction lever to neutral or one side rather than leaving it under tension. It is a small habit, but ratchets that spend years in a damp toolbox with grit in the head are the ones that skip when you most need them to hold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Tooth Count Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap 90-tooth ratchet has finer teeth without the steel and heat treatment to support them, which makes it weaker than a well-made 72-tooth. Judge the pawl, the steel and the warranty first; treat tooth count as a tiebreaker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Ratchet as a Breaker Bar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The commonest way to ruin one. High torque rounds the tooth the pawl is sitting on, and the ratchet skips at that position from then on. Break fasteners loose with a breaker bar, then switch to the ratchet to spin them out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Pipe on the Handle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                All the added leverage lands on a mechanism designed for hand force, and the failure is sudden rather than gradual. A longer breaker bar costs little and is built for exactly this.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chasing 120 Teeth for Open Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Beyond 72 teeth the gains are a degree at a time while the mechanism gets progressively more delicate and less tolerant of dirt. Unless your work is genuinely confined, that trade is the wrong way round.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Cleaning the Mechanism</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grit inside the head grinds the teeth it is meant to engage. One annual strip, clean and regrease is the difference between a ratchet that lasts decades and one that starts skipping in its third year.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does ratchet tooth count mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the number of teeth on the gear inside the ratchet head, and it sets how far you must swing the handle before the pawl catches the next tooth. Divide 360 by the tooth count to get the swing arc: 72 teeth need 5 degrees, 90 teeth need 4, 36 teeth need 10.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 90-tooth ratchet better than a 72-tooth?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In confined spaces, marginally — it needs 4 degrees of swing instead of 5. But the finer teeth are smaller and the mechanism more delicate, so a well-made 72-tooth ratchet outperforms a cheap 90-tooth one. The big jump is from 36 to 72; after that the returns diminish quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good tooth count for a ratchet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seventy-two teeth suits most people: a 5-degree swing handles the overwhelming majority of tight spots while keeping the teeth substantial. Move to 90 if you regularly work in engine bays or machinery. Stay at 36 if your access is generally open and you value robustness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do more teeth make a ratchet weaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Each tooth is smaller, with less material at its base and less depth for the pawl to engage, so under very high torque a fine-tooth ratchet is more likely to skip. Good manufacturers offset this with better steel and pawl design, but a cheap high-count ratchet has the fine teeth without that compensation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my ratchet skip under load?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually a rounded tooth from being used as a breaker bar, or grit and dried grease preventing the pawl from seating fully. Strip and clean the head first, since that fixes many cases. If it skips at one particular position, a tooth is damaged and the ratchet needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a breaker bar instead of a ratchet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For breaking fasteners loose, yes, always. A breaker bar has no mechanism to skip or damage, and it keeps the high-torque work away from the part of your kit most likely to fail. Then use the ratchet for what it does well: spinning the fastener out quickly once it is moving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I maintain a ratchet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Once a year, remove the cover on the back of the head, clean out the old grease and grit, and repack with a light grease. Keep it dry, never extend the handle with a pipe, and do not use it to break fasteners loose. Most ratchets that fail were abused rather than worn out.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

